<h1>how do i turn my side project into something that counts as work experience</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">You make it legible to recruiters. Most side projects die in the description because they read like hobbies, not decisions with consequences. Reframe them as scored proof of specific skills used in real roles, and recruiters will treat them as work experience.</p>

<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li>Resume use fell to 67% in 2025, down from 73% the year before, as employers hunt for proof beyond the page, <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla State of Skills-Based Hiring 2025</a></li>
<li>53% of employers have dropped degree requirements, up 77% year-over-year, but fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected real change, <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla 2025</a> and <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Burning Glass Institute + HBS</a></li>
<li>Internship-to-full-time conversion sits at 63.1%, the single most influential factor between equally qualified candidates, <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">NACE</a></li>
<li>The gap between "I built something" and "hire me" is not the project, it is the scoring layer that translates it into recruiter language</li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
<h2>why most side projects fail the recruiter test</h2>

<p>The project itself is rarely the problem. The framing is.</p>

<p>Recruiters scan for three signals: scope, stakes, and skill match. Most side project descriptions hit none. "Built a personal finance app in React" tells a recruiter you have free time and a tutorial completion streak, not whether you made architectural decisions under constraint, whether anyone else used it, or whether you iterated after failure.</p>

<p>The filter operating system of school trained you to list activities, but employers need decision records. A course teaches you syntax, while a shipped project with user complaints, a pivot, and a performance bottleneck you solved becomes a decision record with consequences, the translation layer most people skip.</p>

<p>The honest reason side projects get dismissed: the person reading your resume has seen "passion project" code that never ran in production, never had a user, never required a tradeoff. They are not wrong to be skeptical. Your job is to make the skepticism impossible.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>the four elements that make self-directed work credible</h2>

<p>Recruiters who actually hire from side projects look for specific evidence. Build your description around these four:</p>

<p><strong>Constraint.</strong> What limited you? Time, money, a platform's API limits, a team member who quit? "Built in 72 hours for a hackathon deadline" or "Rewrote after first version crashed at 100 concurrent users" shows engineering under pressure, not leisure coding.</p>

<p><strong>Stake.</strong> Who besides you cared about the outcome? Real users, a client who paid, a community that complained? "Open-sourced and got 14 issues filed" beats "available on GitHub." Money is the clearest stake, but attention works too. If nobody noticed, say what you did to try to make them notice and why it failed. Honesty about failed distribution is still a decision record.</p>

<p><strong>Sequence.</strong> What did you do first, what did you abandon, and why? "Started with Firebase, hit scaling limits, migrated to PostgreSQL in week three" shows you can throw away sunk cost. The filter OS punishes failure. Professional work requires it.</p>

<p><strong>Skill match.</strong> Do not list every technology you touched. Match the job's stated requirements with specific project moments. If they want "cross-functional collaboration," describe the argument with your designer friend about navigation hierarchy. If they want "product sense," describe the feature you cut because usage data showed nobody clicked it.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>how to write the project so it reads like a job</h2>

<p>The structure that works is not "what I built" but "what problem I owned, what I tried, what happened."</p>

<p>Bad: "Created a web scraper to track apartment prices." Good: "Noticed friends were manually checking 12 rental sites; built a scraper that reduced search time from 3 hours to 10 minutes; hit Craigslist's rate limit in week two, switched to cached polling architecture; 23 friends used it for 4 months, then I shut it down when a paid competitor launched and I analyzed why their UX won."</p>

<p>The second version has scope (rental market, not "web scraper"), stakes (friends depended on it), sequence (rate limit → architecture change), and a real ending (shutdown, competitive analysis). That is a product manager's decision record, and it is also an engineer's: the same project, two different skill matches, depending on the role.</p>

<p>Use numbers that mean something to the role. Engineers: latency, throughput, error rate reduction. Marketers: acquisition cost, conversion funnel, retention. Designers: task completion time, error rate in user tests, iterations after feedback. Generic "grew to 500 users" is weak unless you say how and at what cost.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>where to put it so recruiters actually see it</h2>

<p>The resume is not the only channel, and for scored work it is increasingly not the best one.</p>

<p>Recruiters spend seconds on resumes because they are low-signal documents. A GitHub link in a resume line is a gesture, not evidence. The recruiter will not click it. The hiring manager might, if the resume already passed filters.</p>

<p>The better path: put the proof where the recruiter already works. LinkedIn projects are barely better than resume lines. A short Loom walkthrough of the project, posted where the role's community actually gathers (Substack, Twitter/X, relevant Discord, industry Slack), builds ambient credibility. The ideal is a scored submission against a professional bar, something the recruiter can open and see ranked against other candidates, which functions like the Google ads algorithm for people: your work surfaces when the role matches, not when you beg for attention.</p>

<p>If you must use the traditional resume, give the project its own section titled "Independent Work" or "Self-Directed Projects," not "Hobbies." Put it above unrelated internships. Order by relevance to the role, not chronology.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>when a side project is not enough (and what to add)</h2>

<p>Some gaps a project cannot close alone. Know them honestly.</p>

<p>Collaboration is the hardest to fake solo. If the role requires "leading cross-functional teams," one project where you argued with a friend is thin. Supplement with open-source contribution where you navigated maintainers' feedback, or explicit documentation of how you sought out users, handled complaints, or coordinated with a remote collaborator.</p>

<p>Scale is the other gap. A project used by 20 people is real work, but it is not the same constraint as serving 20,000. Be honest about scale, then show what you did to prepare for it: load testing, architecture decisions made with growth in mind, the feature you deferred to keep performance stable.</p>

<p>The final gap is external validation, where self-reported skill is noise and scored work against a standard the employer recognizes is signal. The old path collects credentials and hopes someone notices, while the new one builds proof and gets matched to the work that actually wants you.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
<h2>frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>How long does a side project need to run to count as experience?</h3>
<p>Duration matters less than density of decisions. A two-week sprint with real users, failure, and iteration beats six months of occasional tinkering with no stakes. Signal the intensity, not the calendar time.</p>

<h3>Can I count a project I built for a class or bootcamp?</h3>
<p>Only if you extended it beyond the assignment. "Built what the instructor specified" is coursework. "Used the class project as a base, then added a feature based on user feedback, deployed it, and got 50 signups" is self-directed work with a starting constraint. The extension is what counts.</p>

<h3>What if my project failed completely?</h3>
<p>Failure with documentation is stronger than success with none. Describe the hypothesis, the test, the result, and what you would do differently. Shutdown analysis is standard professional practice. Most candidates hide failure. You will stand out by owning it with specificity.</p>

<h3>Should I list technologies or outcomes?</h3>
<p>Outcomes first, technologies only as evidence for the outcome. "React, Node, MongoDB" is a stack. "Reduced load time from 4.2s to 800ms by switching from MongoDB aggregations to cached Redis queries" is a decision record that happens to name technologies.</p>

<h3>How do I handle a project where I was the only user?</h3>
<p>Be honest and add distribution attempts. "Built for personal use, then posted on Reddit and got 12 downloads, 3 angry emails about crashes, fixed the top bug" is stronger than pretending you had users. The attempt and response to feedback is the work experience.</p>
</section>

